<%# Re-renders every timestamp on the page in the READER's own timezone.

  PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT, and that direction is the point. The server renders a
  complete, labelled UTC timestamp; this replaces the text with the same instant
  in the reader's zone, also labelled. With no JavaScript, a blocked script or a
  CSP that rejects this tag, the screen still shows a correct timestamp -- which
  is why the server does not render an empty element for the script to fill.

  `Intl.DateTimeFormat` and nothing else. A date library on an audit screen would
  be a dependency in every adopting application, for arithmetic the platform has
  done since 2017. `datetime` holds the exact recorded instant at microsecond
  precision and `title` keeps it visible on hover, so the stored fact survives
  whatever the display says.

  THE FIELD SET IS THE LIBRARY'S; the CONVENTIONS are not. Date, year, time and
  zone name are always all present, because that is what the display bug fixed in
  §4 was about. Field order, month name, digit shape and the 12-or-24-hour clock
  come from `config.timestamp_locale`, or from the reader's own locale when that
  is nil. A host wanting a fixed clock as well as a fixed order spells it in the
  tag -- "en-US-u-hc-h23" is American order on a 24-hour clock.

  A BAD TAG FALLS BACK TO THE READER'S LOCALE, not to nothing. `Intl` throws
  RangeError on a malformed tag, and bailing out there would leave every
  timestamp in UTC with nothing said -- so the second `try` is the difference
  between a wrong setting looking wrong and looking like this feature is off.
  Configuration#verify_display_time_zone! rejects the obvious typos at boot.

  `nonce: true` supplies the host's CSP nonce when it has a policy and omits the
  attribute when it does not. Without it, an app with a strict policy loses the
  enhancement silently.

  Rendered from `shared/_nav`, so it arrives once on every screen and no screen
  has to remember it. It runs immediately AND on `turbo:load`, because the nav is
  at the top of the document: on a first load the timestamps below it are not
  parsed yet, while a Turbo navigation inserts the whole body before scripts run.
  The listeners are registered once per window, or a long Turbo session would
  accumulate one per navigation.
%>
<%= javascript_tag nonce: true do %>
  (function () {
    var opts = {
      day: "2-digit", month: "short", year: "numeric",
      hour: "2-digit", minute: "2-digit", timeZoneName: "short"
    };
    var locale = <%= AuditLog.config.timestamp_locale.to_json.html_safe %>, f;

    try {
      f = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale || undefined, opts);
    } catch (e) {
      try { f = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, opts); } catch (e2) { return; }
    }

    function run() {
      var nodes = document.querySelectorAll('time[data-audit-time="local"]');
      for (var i = 0; i < nodes.length; i++) {
        var el = nodes[i], d = new Date(el.getAttribute("datetime"));
        if (isNaN(d.getTime())) continue;
        el.textContent = f.format(d);
        el.setAttribute("data-audit-time", "done");
      }
    }

    run();

    if (!window.__auditLogLocalTime) {
      window.__auditLogLocalTime = true;
      document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", run);
      document.addEventListener("turbo:load", run);
      document.addEventListener("turbo:frame-render", run);
    }
  })();
<% end %>
